/*
 * The globals the compiler fills - direction 4 of `role.h`.
 *
 * A handful of the library's globals are not ordinary declarations. The compiler stores into one
 * before the program's first instruction runs, or decides what another holds at all - and either
 * direction needs the compiler and the library to be talking about the same declaration.
 *
 * The way that used to be arranged was a name written out in the backend: `commandLineCount` spelled
 * in two code generators, matching a `pub let` in `lib/Native/Linux.x64.yana` that nothing checked.
 * Renaming the library's global compiled cleanly and produced a program whose arguments were
 * silently zero, on both backends, with no diagnostic anywhere. That is the failure this table
 * exists to make impossible, and it is the failure the other four tables in this directory were
 * built to close for the rest of the surface.
 *
 * The link is written in the source, as an attribute:
 *
 *     @builtin(commandLineCount)
 *     pub let &commandLineCount = 0 :: Size
 *
 * The declaration's own name, its module, its `pub` and its file are the library's business and may
 * all change without the compiler noticing; what the compiler will not do is fail to notice a role
 * that is not in this table, or two declarations claiming one role, or a role claimed by a
 * declaration of the wrong shape.
 *
 * **A role no declaration claims is simply unfilled**, and that is the ordinary case rather than an
 * error: a program that never asks about its command line does not reach those globals, they are
 * never emitted, and the entry sequence writes nothing.
 *
 * ## The columns
 *
 *  - `role` is the enumerator, the attribute's spelling, and the order of the enum.
 *  - `kind` is which way the fact travels - `BuiltinKind`.
 *  - `shape` is what the declaration's type has to be, coarsely - `BuiltinShape`.
 *
 * ## The order is load-bearing, once
 *
 * `Program::builtins` and `LowerModule::builtins` are arrays indexed by this enum, which is
 * ordinary. What is not ordinary is that the LLVM backend reads the command-line three as `main`'s
 * parameters 0, 1 and 2 - so those three stay first and stay in `argc`, `argv`, `envp` order.
 * `kCommandLineGlobals` is what says so, and it is stated so that a role added below cannot quietly
 * become a fourth parameter of `main`.
 */

/*
 * What the process was started with - Design-Test.md §11.2's F4.
 *
 * Written by the entry sequence before anything else runs; `lib/Environment` is what reads them
 * afterwards.
 */
//                 role                     kind                   shape
YANA_SUPPLIED_ROLE( commandLineCount,        BuiltinKind::Written,  BuiltinShape::Word    )
YANA_SUPPLIED_ROLE( commandLineValues,       BuiltinKind::Written,  BuiltinShape::Address )
YANA_SUPPLIED_ROLE( commandLineEnvironment,  BuiltinKind::Written,  BuiltinShape::Address )

/*
 * The target's mapping granularity, as a compile-time constant - `Native/Memory.native.yana`.
 *
 * **A lower bound and not the page the kernel is running with**: what is stated here is the smallest
 * granularity the target guarantees, which is what a mapping may be rounded up to and what a tail
 * guard may be sized from. Both stay correct when the kernel's page is larger, since the kernel
 * rounds the mapping again. A caller that needs the exact number asks the kernel for it -
 * `systemPageBytes`, out of the auxiliary vector - and that is a different question.
 *
 * It replaced the same 4096 written in two library files, one of which was `Heap.native.yana`'s: a
 * statement about a kernel made by a file selected on `native`, which has no platform to be right
 * about.
 */
YANA_SUPPLIED_ROLE( pageBytes,               BuiltinKind::Supplied, BuiltinShape::Word    )

/*
 * How many bytes the target's natural vector holds - `Core/Vector.yana`.
 *
 * Sixteen for SSE2, NEON and JavaScript, thirty-two at x86-64's v3. It is the same fact
 * `lanes(zero() :: Vec(U8))` answers, and that is what it replaced - seven copies of that expression
 * across `Native/Memory.native.yana` and `Core/Text.native.yana`, each with a comment beside it
 * saying that the reader wanted a constant and not a call.
 *
 * **A byte count and not a lane count**, because the lane count is a question about a lane type and
 * this is a question about a register. `Vec(a)`'s lanes are this divided by what one `a` occupies,
 * which the language can already work out; what it has no other way to say is the register's own
 * width as a number a program may compute with.
 */
YANA_SUPPLIED_ROLE( vectorBytes,             BuiltinKind::Supplied, BuiltinShape::Word    )

/*
 * How many bits the target's word has - `Core/Bits.yana`, and the width of `Size` and `USize`.
 *
 * Analysis-Modules.md's Move 2 made those two primitives whose width the *target* answers, which is
 * what lets a 32-bit native target exist and is why nothing above `repr` may assume 64. The cost of
 * that is a body which genuinely needs the number - one that assembles a word out of 32-bit pieces,
 * or masks against its top - and until this role there was no way to say it.
 *
 * **There is a way to say it in source, and that is the reason for a role rather than an argument
 * against one.** `leadingZeros(0 :: USize)` is the type's own width and folds to a constant. The
 * near miss is what this replaces: the obvious spelling, `bitWidth(value)`, is the *value's*
 * significant bits, so it compiles, runs, and quietly answers 32 about a 64-bit word.
 *
 * Bits and not bytes, where `vectorBytes` above is bytes. A vector's byte count is what a caller
 * computes addresses with and `sizeOf` already answers for anything; a word's *bit* count is what a
 * shift distance and a mask are written in, and dividing by eight at every use is how a reader stops
 * trusting the name.
 */
YANA_SUPPLIED_ROLE( wordBits,                BuiltinKind::Supplied, BuiltinShape::Word    )

/*
 * Which end of a word the target puts the low byte at - `Core/Bits.yana`'s `Target.byteOrder`.
 *
 * `wordBits`' argument at the other fact a word has, and it arrived the same way. This used to be a
 * *global the compiler invented*: `addTargetConstant` in resolve/core.cpp built a `Target.byteOrder`
 * out of nothing, under a name written in C++, answering a `byteorder` metric that a target folded.
 * That is the arrangement this whole directory exists to remove - a name in the compiler matching a
 * declaration nothing checks - except that here there was no declaration at all, so `Core` could not
 * even say what the constant's type was without the compiler agreeing by hand.
 *
 * As a supplied role it is an ordinary `pub let` of the library's own `Endian`, and the compiler
 * replaces what it holds. Everything downstream then treats it as what it is, an immutable global
 * with a constant initializer - so `Target.byteOrder == Big` still folds to nothing and the arm it
 * guards is still gone before any machine sees it, which was the metric's whole purpose.
 *
 * `BuiltinShape::Enum` is here for this row and no other: the two answers are a *library* type with
 * two constructors, and neither a word nor an address is what a backend would store.
 */
YANA_SUPPLIED_ROLE( byteOrder,               BuiltinKind::Supplied, BuiltinShape::Enum    )
